                <pb facs="0156" n="22" sameAs="frus:frus1888p1/pg22"/>

                <head>No. 21.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Lawton to Mr. Bayard.</hi></head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Extract.]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Legation of the United States</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Vienna</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1888-01-06"><hi rendition="#italic">January</hi> 6, 1888</date>.
                        (Received January 31.)</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 25.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: In view of the anti-Semitic imbroglio which
                    somewhat excited the Governments of Austria-Hungary and the United States, not
                    long since, it is interesting to note that during the recent “holidays” the
                    Baron and Baroness Albert Rothschild were declared by imperial decree <hi
                        rendition="#italic">hoffähig</hi>, that is to say, they will for the future
                    be admitted to court balls. This is the first time that such a privilege has
                    ever been conceded to persons of Jewish origin or faith, and it is causing a
                    great sensation in the highest society of Vienna. Very many quarterings of
                    nobility (sixteen, I believe) are the usual requisites of <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Hoffähigkeit;</hi> and it was not until last year that
                    the wives of cabinet ministers, not being members of noble families, were
                    admitted to court by reason of their husbands’ offices.</p>
                <p>This exclusion from court circles has long been sorely felt by the Rothschild
                    family, and it has been rumored during the past year that Baron Albert intended
                    to dispose of his palace residence and other property in Vienna, and retire
                    altogether from Austria with his colossal fortune.</p>
                <p>I have, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">A. R.
                        Lawton.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
